  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to minimize the AC1 upper bound by simultaneously optimizing sequence length and structure. Unlike previous approaches that fix the sequence length, this method explores multiple length scales through progressive interpolation and hybridization. The core innovation is the use of a physics-inspired optimization that combines soft thresholding with curvature-aware updates, enabling efficient exploration of both sparse and complex structures.\n\nJustification: The AC1 optimization landscape exhibits multiple local minima, making traditional gradient-free methods struggle with convergence. This approach addresses this by:\n- Dynamically adjusting degrees of freedom (length) based on problem characteristics\n- Balancing exploration and exploitation through adaptive neighborhood structures\n- Leveraging soft constraints to maintain mathematical continuity\n\nLimitations: While highly effective for sequences with smooth or piecewise structures, it may underperform for extremely oscillatory solutions. The convex-concave envelope approach deliberately trades local optimization for broader explorability.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- **Representation**: The sequence is represented as a superposition: 10% analytic Gaussian basis functions + 40% localized trigonometric modes, normalized with unit energy.\n- **Cost function**: J(a) = max_k (a* a)[k] / (sum(a))^2\n- **Constraints**: 0 \u2264 a_i \u2264 1000, \u2211a_i \u2265 0.01\n- **Algorithm**: Progressive interpolation with curvature-adaptive penalty (\u03a0)\n  - Initialize with Gaussian or uniform pulses\n  - Compute gradient/curvature using Fourier basis\n  - Apply soft thresholding with adaptive threshold \u03b8\n  - Update using curvature-aware step size\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitializations:\n- Sample base sequences from exponential/periodic/Gaussian families\n- Scale lengths: L \u2208 [60,120,180,240] with interpolation quality factor \u03b1 [0.8,1.2]\n Optimization phase:\n- Apply wavelet-moded Fourier continuation\n- Compute score via exact numpy convolution\n- Update vector using transformed gradient:\n\np(i) \u2190 Clamp[0,1000] (p(i) + \u03bc * \u2207J + \u03b7 * (\u0394\u00b2J - \u03b3))\n\n- Local search using Nelder-Mead simplex variants at \u03b8% points (\u03b8=5-20%)\n- Use exponential moving average smoothing for gradient diversity\n\n4. Search choices\n- Auto-tuning:\n  - Length resolution: logarithmic scale sampling\n  - Mode mixing ratio: based on stationarity index S\n  - Penalization factor: adaptive based on {entropy, curvature}\n- Restart policy:\n  - Divert to trigonometric basis if block-based gradient dominance > 0.6\n  - Exploit periodicity if stationarity < -1.2\n- Neighborhood adaptations:\n  - Wavelet shrinkage with parameter \u03b2 [0.8]\n\n5. Computation budget\n- Baseline: 60-length sequences (cost: O(n log n))\n- Active monitoring at 10% checkpoints\n- Deadline checks via timer interrupts at solver boundaries\n- Stochastic fallback for stuck optima\n\n6. Validation contract\n- Exact score verification via numpy convolution at 3 scales\n- Contour integration to estimate AC1 bounds\n- Fallback generator:\n\ndef fallback(n, xs, ys):\n    if n <= 10:\n        return np.exp(-np.arange(n)**2/(2*5)) * 300\n    else:\n        return np.log(np.exp(np.arange(n)*(np.log(3)-np.log(1000)) )",
